Prader-Willi syndrome
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Neonatal hypotonia/poor feeding, Childhood hyperphagia/obesity, Small hands and feet, Hypogonadism

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-039
Reason for Visit: Acute severe abdominal pain, vomiting, and suspected gastric distension.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a 10-year-old child with Prader-Willi syndrome who suddenly developed severe, agonizing abdominal pain, repeated episodes of non-bilious vomiting, and a visibly distended, rigid abdomen following an unwitnessed binge eating episode at a birthday party. Assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the life-threatening risk of acute gastric necrosis or perforation, a known lethal complication of extreme hyperphagia in this syndrome, requiring immediate clinical evaluation for surgical referral.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: His stomach is huge and rock hard, he is crying in agony, and he keeps throwing up undigested food.
History of Present Illness: The parents report a 2-hour history of acute, severe abdominal distress. The patient has a known diagnosis of Prader-Willi syndrome characterized by profound hyperphagia, insatiable appetite, and early-onset morbid obesity. Two hours ago, the patient attended a party and bypassed parental supervision, consuming a massive, unquantified volume of food rapidly. Subsequently, the patient developed extreme, generalized abdominal pain and a visibly bloated stomach. He has vomited three times, bringing up large amounts of completely undigested food. The parents note the patient has a very high pain tolerance usually, making his current severe distress highly alarming.
Functional Status: Ambulant; completely dependent on caregivers for strict environmental food security.
Social/Safety Concerns: Imminent risk of gastric rupture; failure of environmental supervision.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 125 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 26 bpm | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 75.0 Kg (Morbid obesity)
General: Child is intensely distressed, groaning, pale, and diaphoretic. Characteristic small hands and feet are noted.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals massive, tense, tympanic distension primarily in the epigastric and umbilical regions. The abdomen is exquisitely tender to light palpation globally, with profound voluntary guarding. Rebound tenderness is equivocal due to extreme distress and body habitus. Bowel sounds are absent. Cardiovascular exam shows a regular tachycardia indicative of severe pain and potential early hypovolemia from third-spacing. Respiratory exam reveals rapid, shallow breathing due to diaphragmatic splinting from the massive gastric distension. Known hypogonadism (cryptorchidism) is documented in the history.
Environmental Observations: N/A - The binge event occurred externally, but the sheer volume consumed indicates a complete breakdown of supervision.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications relevant to the acute presentation.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Prader-Willi syndrome (complicated by acute massive gastric dilation and suspected impending necrosis) - Status: Acute, critical complication
Differentials:

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause due to the massive, generalized, tympanic distension originating in the epigastrium immediately following a binge event, rather than localized right lower quadrant peritonitis.

Viral Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely given the extreme, rigid abdominal distension, the absence of diarrhea, and the specific mechanical history of a massive binge-eating event.

Cholecystitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the global, tympanic nature of the distension and pain, rather than isolated right upper quadrant tenderness with a positive Murphy's sign.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Surgical emergency)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a critical surgical emergency: massive gastric dilation following extreme hyperphagic binge eating. The objective findings of a tense, tympanic, exquisitely tender abdomen, absent bowel sounds, and tachycardia in a patient with an intrinsically high pain threshold confirmed an impending catastrophic event. This assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period because the stomach wall in Prader-Willi patients can rapidly undergo ischemic necrosis and fatal rupture due to massive acute distension. Immediate emergency transport to a pediatric surgical center for gastric decompression (NG tube placement) and urgent imaging was absolutely mandatory.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Maintained the patient strictly nil by mouth. Positioned the patient in a semi-upright posture to reduce the risk of massive aspiration if further vomiting occurred. Administered a micro-dose of intranasal fentanyl for temporary pain relief to facilitate safe transport, acknowledging that definitive relief requires mechanical decompression. Activated 000 for immediate emergency ambulance transport to the Paediatric Emergency Department.
Positive Behavioral Change: N/A - Acute life-threatening emergency requiring immediate transfer.
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A.
Follow-up: Handover provided directly to the responding paramedic crew and receiving pediatric surgical registrar.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Patient is currently being transported to the Emergency Department via ambulance under my direct supervision.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Gastric necrosis in Prader-Willi syndrome has a high mortality rate; immediate surgical evaluation is critical.
Clinical Course: The patient will require immediate placement of a large-bore nasogastric tube in the ED to suction out the stomach contents and relieve the pressure, followed by CT imaging to ensure the stomach wall has not already torn.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not allow the child to drink any water or take any oral medications. The stomach is completely full and cannot empty, leading to severe risk of inhaling vomit.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Because his brain never tells him he is full, he ate an amount of food that has stretched his stomach to the point where the blood vessels are being crushed. This is causing the agonizing pain and risks his stomach tearing open. The hospital team needs to put a tube down his throat immediately to pump the food out and save his stomach.
Actionable Steps: Pack his medical records detailing his Prader-Willi diagnosis. Ensure the hospital team understands he has a high pain tolerance, meaning his current pain is a severe emergency. Follow paramedic instructions.

